          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            %em cryptography
            is the art and science of securing communication and information through the use of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematical techniques
          %p
            Its roots trace back to ancient civilizations where secret codes were employed to protect sensitive messages. The earliest known use of cryptography is attributed to the ancient Egyptians, who used
            %a{:href => "https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/brief-history-cryptography"} hieroglyphs to inscribe messages
            on monuments in a way that could only be understood by those with the knowledge of the secret key.
          %p Today, cryptography relies on a wide variety of mathematical topics. Here is a non-exhaustive list of fields and topics that are particularly relevant to cryptography:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Number Theory
                (modular arithmetic, prime numbers, and group theory in the context of modular arithmetic)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Abstract Algebra
                (groups, rings, and fields such as Galois fields)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Algebraic Geometry
                (elliptic curve cryptography and algebraic varieties over finite fields)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Combinatorics
                (combinatorial designs and error-correcting codes)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Probability Theory
                (randomized algorithms in cryptography and probability distributions relevant to cryptographic protocols
            %li
              %p
                %strong Graph Theory
                (graph-based cryptographic algorithms and network flow algorithms in the context of network security)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Topology
                (algebraic topology and homological algebra)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Logic
                (in the context of formal verification and proof systems)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Complex Analysis
                (analytic number theory)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Functional Analysis
                (for signal processing and cryptographic protocols)
          %h2#symmetric_and_asymmetric_cryptography Symmetric and asymmetric cryptography
          %p
            There are two fundamental types of cryptography:
            %em symmetric
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em asymmetric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            for both encryption
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_k:M \to C
            and decryption
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_k:C \to M
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "center center center center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Bob
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Channel
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Alice
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi m
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi D
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{ccccc}
                  \\mathsf{Bob} &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \mathsf{Channel} &amp; \longrightarrow &amp;\mathsf{Alice}\\
                  m &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; E_k(m) &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; D_{k}(E_k(m)) = m
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            On the other hand, asymmetric cryptography uses a pair of keys – a public key
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            for encryption
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_k:M \to C
            and a private key
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k'
            for decryption
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{k'}:C \to M
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "center center center center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Bob
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Channel
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Alice
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi m
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi D
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{ccccc}
                  \\mathsf{Bob} &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \mathsf{Channel} &amp; \longrightarrow &amp;\mathsf{Alice}\\
                  m &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; E_k(m) &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; D_{k'}(E_k(m)) = m
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            In practice, Alice would generate both keys
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k'
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            and would send the public key
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            to Bob.
          %h2#security_and_attacks Security and attacks
          %p Security in cryptography is a constant battle against potential attacks. Common attacks include brute force, where an attacker systematically tries all possible keys, and cryptanalysis, which involves exploiting vulnerabilities in the cryptographic algorithm.
          %p Modern cryptographic systems aim to withstand these attacks, often relying on the complexity of mathematical problems, such as factoring large numbers or solving discrete logarithms, to ensure security.
          %h3#complexity_classes Complexity classes
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexity+class"} Complexity classes
            play a pivotal role in cryptography, providing a framework to analyze the efficiency and security of cryptographic algorithms.
          %p The study of computational complexity helps assess the computational resources required to break a cryptographic scheme, distinguishing between feasible and infeasible attacks.
          %h3#invertibility_principle Invertibility principle
          %p
            In cryptography, the concept of invertibility is essential for ensuring secure decryption. When plaintext undergoes encryption through the encryption algorithm
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_k:M \to C
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} Bijections
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E:M \to C
            whose inverses
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = E^{-1}:C \to M
            are hard to compute provide practical examples of this principle. Such bijections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,D)
            establish a one-to-one relationship between plaintext
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and ciphertext
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Mathematically, we could imagine that Alice and Bob have access to a groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            and a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is embedded in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            via an inclusion functor.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{C} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{G}
          %p The security of a cryptosystem would then rely on the difficulty of reconstructing this inclusion using an algorithmic completion procedure.
          %p These mathematical principles form the foundation for secure cryptosystems, ensuring that only authorized users can successfully decrypt and access sensitive information.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Note that there is flexibility in considering weaker notions of isomorphisms, especially if Alice can employ these weaker isomorphisms effectively in the decryption of the message
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              from the data
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{k'}(E_k(m))
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇆
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D_{k'}(E_k(m)) \leftrightarrows m
            %p
              In category theory, examples of these weaker notions can be derived from higher category theory. This includes concepts such as
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalences
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+for+an+endofunctor"} algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebras"} coalgebras
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retractions"} retractions
          %h2#postquantum_cryptography Post-quantum cryptography
          %p
            With the advent of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cryptography"} quantum cryptography
            is an evolving field that explores algorithms resistant to quantum attacks.
          %p Researchers are developing cryptographic schemes that rely on mathematical problems difficult for both classical and quantum computers to solve, ensuring the continued security of communication in the post-quantum era.
          %h3#useful_links Useful links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
